 "guide": {
  "kazanim": [
   "TAEOB.3 (unverified) Showing phonological awareness skills: notices the first sounds of words; matches words that start with the same sound, such as apple–Ali, zero–Zeynep, drum–Deniz.",
   "TAOB.2 Making meaning from visual materials: works out whose picture symbol card is whose from the picture and the first sound; connects the card with their own symbol in the classroom, that is, with their daily life.",
   "TADB.3 Analysing a story they hear: retells the events of losing, searching, finding and sticking back in the order they happened.",
   "SDB1.1 Self-Awareness: recognises their name, the first sound of their name and their own symbol; notices and names how sadness feels in the body (a lump in the throat) and tries deep breathing and asking for help.",
   "D4 Friendship: notices a friend's problem and looks for a solution together with them."
  ],
  "once": [
   "Show the cover: ask “What might Ali be looking for? A real apple, or something else?”",
   "Look at the symbol cards in your classroom together: “What is on your card? What sound does that picture start with?”",
   "Introduce the sound-stretching game: “Tttable, sssun.” The children stretch the first sound together with you."
  ],
  "sirasi": [
   "On page 1, say “Eeela, eeelephant” and stretch the first sound; the children stretch it with you. Introduce Zeynep and Deniz too.",
   "On page 2, name Ali's feeling and take a deep breath together.",
   "For the prediction question on page 3, accept both answers; the cards will turn up in both places.",
   "On page 5, before matching, give a clue by stretching the first sound: “Zzzero… Zzzeynep!”; for sounds that can't be stretched, like D, repeat it: “d-d-drum.” Let the child do the touching."
  ],
  "sonra": [
   "How did Ali feel when he couldn't see his card? What happened in his body? What did he do next?",
   "How did Ela work out whose cards they were?",
   "What sound does your name start with? Can you say something that starts with that sound?",
   "Let's put the story in order with the pictures: what happened first, and what happened next?"
  ],
  "sinif": {
   "ad": "My Symbol Card",
   "sure": "20 min",
   "materyal": "Card, crayons, tape, various objects from the classroom (apple, bell, ball, book, scissors, etc.)",
   "adim": [
    "Make a circle. Each child stretches the first sound as they say their name: “Aaali”, “Eeela”, “Zzzeynep”.",
    "From the objects in the middle, each child finds one that starts with the first sound of their name (Ben–book, Sam–scissors). For names with a harder first sound (Ursula–umbrella, Yasmin–yo-yo), come up with ideas together.",
    "Each child draws a picture of the object they chose on their card; write the name under the card for any child who wants it.",
    "Stick the cards firmly onto the cubbies or coat pegs. The next morning, mix the cards up and play “Whose card is this?”"
   ]
  },
  "evSoru": [
   "How did Ali feel when his card went missing? How would you feel?",
   "How did Ela find the cards? How did the first sounds help her?",
   "What sound does your name start with? What things at home start with that sound?"
  ],
  "evEtk": {
   "ad": "The Picture Card on My Door",
   "d": "With your child, choose a picture that starts with the first sound of their name (such as Deniz–drum, Ela–elephant). Draw it together and hang it on their bedroom door. Make a card for everyone in the family too; during the day, play “What sound does this picture start with, and whose door is it?”"
  },
  "aileNotu": "Today in class we read “Where Is Ali's Apple?” In the story, Ela returned the lost picture cards to their owners by listening to the first sounds of words: apple–Ali, zero–Zeynep, drum–Deniz. We talked with the children about which sound our names start with. At home you can go on a sound hunt with everyday objects by asking “What sound does this start with?”, saying the sounds stretched out (tttable) rather than the letter names."
 },
